+  "title": "GOV: badf-security in solution-design routing names a family that never existed; generalize #222's registry-resolution guard with a declared-future allowlist",
+  "problem": "\ud83d\udd0e **REV (BADF-REV)** \u2014 surfaced while reviewing #222 (WP-2026-0109). Out of that PR's scope \u2014 different family surface \u2014 so it is filed rather than fixed in-PR.\n\n#222 adds a guard that every `badf-*` token under `skills/badf-build/**` resolves in the registry, after `badf-verification (G08)` sat in badf-build's frozen Boundary block naming a family that does not exist. I ran that guard's logic across **every** family surface. Two distinct classes came back, and only one is a defect.\n\n## The defect \u2014 `badf-security` in `badf-solution-design`\n\n`skills/badf-solution-design/references/routing.md:21`:\n\n```\n| security threat / risk decision | future **`badf-security`** |\n```\n\n`badf-security` is **not a registry family and never has been**. It is a third name matching neither of the two that exist:\n\n- `badf-security-design` \u2014 **`ACTIVE` in the registry today**, and it owns exactly \"security threat / risk decision\".\n- `badf-security-assurance` \u2014 the declared-future assurance family (SAST/SCA/secrets/IaC), consistently labelled \"named, not built\".\n\nSo the routing table sends a live concern to a *future* family when the **ACTIVE** family that owns it already exists. This is the same class as the #219 defect and has a sharper practical consequence: a reader following the table defers a decision that is routable today. My reading is `badf-security-design`, but the owning seat should confirm the intent rather than take my inference.\n\n## Not a defect \u2014 `badf-security-assurance`\n\nAppears in `badf-engineering-verification` (4 files) and `badf-security-design` (6 files), and resolves nowhere \u2014 **correctly**. It is explicitly \"named, not built\" at every use (`GITHUB_CONTROL_PLANE.md:912`, `badf-engineering-verification/SKILL.md:69` and `:131`, routed with declared non-coverage). A naive repo-wide generalization of #222's guard would flag all ten of these and invite someone to delete a deliberate forward reference \u2014 the exact false-positive shape that #210's original criterion had.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `routing.md:21` routes the security threat / risk decision row to the family that actually owns it (`badf-security-design` unless the owner says otherwise); no other row changed.\n2. The #222 guard generalizes to **all** `skills/<family>/**` surfaces, with a **declared-future allowlist** rather than a bare exclusion set: a token may be unresolved only if the same surface labels it \"named, not built\" (or equivalent) at that use. Encode the label check \u2014 an allowlist of bare names would let a genuine typo hide behind a listed name.\n3. Failing-first: with `routing.md:21` unfixed, the generalized guard **fails and names the file and token**; after the fix it passes; and an injected unresolved token in any other family surface also fails (positive control asserted in the test).\n4. The ten `badf-security-assurance` uses stay green throughout \u2014 the allowlist must not be satisfiable by simply listing the name.\n\n**Version tier:** patch. **Lean mode:** advisory. Out of scope: building `badf-security-assurance`; any registry status change.\n\nOwner note: `routing.md` is badf-solution-design's surface (BARCHI-1); the guard generalization touches shared test surface and should come to BADF-QA/BADF-REV.\n\n<!-- agent: reviewer -->\n",
